WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.540
DeepSeq releases official desktop apps for its open source agent harness V0.2 with plug-in management and scheduled tasks. This is Models &amp; Agents, episode 193. DeepSeq released official macOS and Windows desktop apps for its MIT licensed agent harness version 0.2. The apps bundle the DSH command line tool. They support Sinon with a deepSeq account or API key.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:35.040
version 0.2 adds a plugin manager page. It also includes a right hand sidebar for file and code

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:41.980
diff review. Document handling works with spreadsheets and PDFs. An automation task plugin handles

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:48.240
recurring prompts. The harness runs on the cordless paradigm. It supports third-party models through

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:56.480
open AI compatible endpoints. The repository has reached 240,000 GitHub stars. Users can install via

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:06.780
deepseek.com harness or npx at deepseek-ai slash dshweb. The v0.2.1 alpha1 build adds an

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:12.680
experimental Claude code mods compatibility layer. Four session modes are available including

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:19.920
standard, creator, ptc, and minimal. The desktop build eliminates the need for separate node js or

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:26.020
PNPM installs, looking back over the past week, hugging face advanced state verification

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:32.440
for agents, and video rolled out Silicon-level guardrails, and Apple Titan disk access rules

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.500
for agent permissions.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.660
Type-safe AI positions Jev as a smaller model that cannot hallucinate.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.980
The team ran 16,379 benchmark requests measuring latency and billing.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.080
It targets jobs where other models fall short.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.700
The model is built by the co-inventor of chat GPT. Reviewers describe it as

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.960
humbler yet genuinely useful for specific tasks. Altman stated he is very

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.780
uncomfortable with people ascribing religious force or surrendering human

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:08.880
judgment to AI models. He called it a real safety issue. The post received 4.1

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.920
million views. The statement was posted on October 3rd, 2026. Martian presented

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.660
the framework as a route to lower inference costs on frontier models.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.300
The company positions the tool as a practical optimization layer for existing large language

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:24.740
models.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.620
Microsoft Thinking Box grades agents on terminal backend state and side effects instead

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:31.800
of tool call logs.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.960
It runs 507 stateful business workflows 20 times each across various models.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.840
The benchmark is available through hugging face and open end.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:48.020
One retail workflow example showed an agent issuing nine valid calls yet leaving a ticket

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.400
status as solved when the required state was on hold.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:57.320
The approach measures consistency across repeated runs rather than single successful traces.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.080
Nvidia introduced safeguards designed to keep autonomous agents within defined bounds.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:08.500
The safeguards target agents that exceed intended operational limits during execution.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:09.500
5.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.940
Consumer platforms compete with three distinct pricing models.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.380
The platforms target everyday users seeking autonomous task handling.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.240
Agents introduce new vectors for customer data exposure.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.740
The report highlights growing attack surfaces tied to agent autonomy.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:32.500
Simon Willison argues that pay-by-usage services must default to hard-budget caps that return

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.500
errors after a set monthly limit.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:40.920
He notes AWS launched spending limits in September and Google Cloud offers spend caps.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.980
The post recommends an opt-in checkbox for removing the cap.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.700
Coding agents lower the barrier to spinning up services that can incur unexpected charges.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.800
The feature prevents surprise bills from runaway agent activity.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:01.220
Simon Willison notes dot appears limited to a single conversation while he prefers controlling

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.080
context across multiple threads.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.120
The observation follows several days of testing the new interface.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:12.720
It points to AWS and Google Cloud features that pause projects once limits are reached.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.900
Hard limits return errors rather than warnings once the cap is hit.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:21.460
The author prefers errors over surprise multi-thousand dollar bills.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:28.920
Install the DeepSeek Harness V0.2 desktop app on Mac OS or Windows to test the new plugin

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.300
manager and scheduled automation tasks.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:37.440
Run thinking box through hugging face on a sample retail workflow to see how state verification

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:39.420
differs from tool call logging.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.760
Set a monthly spend limit in AWS or Google Cloud before deploying any coding agent that

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:46.960
calls paid APIs.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:54.720
Further compatibility updates expected for deep-seek harness after the V0.2.1 Alpha1 build.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.860
Additional agent safety frameworks from NVIDIA anticipated incoming weeks.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:04.340
more consumer agent platforms likely to announce pricing adjustments.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.820
Pop the hood on state verification and the gap becomes clear right away.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.320
Agent benchmarks that score only tool-call syntax,

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.340
miscases where the final database record is wrong.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:19.400
Thinking box instead replays each workflow 20 times

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.980
and checks the terminal backend state against the required end state.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:25.520
The approach adds measurable overhead

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.660
because every run must reach a stable database snapshot

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.100
rather than stopping at the last function return.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:37.200
This verification layer catches side effects that surface only after the agent declares completion.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:43.600
Teams running long horizon agents should adopt state checks on any workflow that mutates shared records.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:50.320
The cost is higher per evaluation run, but eliminates silent failures that syntax only graders accept.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:56.980
The benchmark isolates each workflow in separate MCP tool sessions to ensure clean state measurement.

